    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d595">

                <pb facs="0702" n="582" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg582"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">815.00 Revolutions/210: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lay</hi>)<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d595-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-07-09T17:00:00-04:00">July 9, 1931—5
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>57. Commander Special Service Squadron reports that conditions on east coast
                    Nicaragua and north coast Honduras are entirely tranquil <pb facs="0703" n="583"
                        xml:id="pg583"/> and therefore recommends withdrawal of the only remaining
                    United States war vessel, the <hi rendition="#italic">Sacramento</hi>, from
                    those regions.</p>
                <p>Please telegraph your views to the Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Castle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d595-f1" n="12" type="source" xml:id="d595-f1-content">
                <p>The same on the same date to the Minister in Nicaragua as Department’s No.
                    152.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
